<!--
    Kuna #1028: a guarded relative jump table is read past its guard, into the
    table that follows it, and the extra rows send flow into the middle of
    instructions (halt_baddata).

    reloaded() is gcc's compact selector dispatch: the guard compares the
    byte in memory, and the index is a second load of the same address.

        cmp     byte ptr [rdi+rsi], 4
        ja      default
        movzx   eax, byte ptr [rdi+rsi]
        lea     rdx, [rip+table_a]
        movsxd  rax, dword ptr [rdx+rax*4]
        add     rax, rdx
        notrack jmp rax

    table_a (5 rows) is immediately followed by neighbour()'s own 8-row
    relative table and then string data.  The two LOADs compute their
    address with separate INT_ADDs, so the guard's range did not apply to
    the index, the byte's full range (256 rows) was emulated, and neighbour's
    rows were read with table_a's base.

    Stage: P2 switch model (guard matching in GuardRecord::value_match).
    A correctness fix, no option: two LOADs through addresses computed the
    same way from the same Varnodes, with no STORE or call between them,
    read one value, so the guard bounds the index to rows 0-4.

    All assertions are on the default pipeline: the five legal rows are
    recovered once each, the switch closes after row 4, nothing reaches an
    instruction interior, and neighbour's own switch is unchanged.
-->
